00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
this is lucy james on behalf of reviewer  alessandra barbosa  battle royals have only been around for  a handful of years  and for the most part they've strictly  stuck within the same genre of games  whether you're playing warzone fortnite  or apex legends you're always shooting a  gun and running from an  encroaching circle four guys ultimate  knockout an extremely  colorful and whimsical battle royale is  a great example of the genre growing  outside of its roots  it's a far more approachable take on the  multiplayer format  with simple controls and a variety of  mini games giving this competitive game  show  as much charm as they do tension  playing as one of 60 multi-colored jelly  bean-shaped contestants  you compete in a joyous and comical race  to be crowned the winner of each of four  guys 15-minute games  each one is broken up by various  mini-games with handfuls of players  eliminated after each one  these games all take on a variety of  themes from straightforward obstacle  race courses to frantic team games where  you're hoarding as many eggs as you can  into a basket each one makes good use of  full guy's easy to understand control  scheme  which lets you jump dive and grab with  ease  the pick up and play nature of full guys  is one of its strongest  aspects along with its quick and  entertaining games  it's never unclear when you need to jump  over a gap or navigate through the  hordes of contestants trying to reach  the finish line before you  its approachability lets anyone have  some fun with it while its stages bring  out glimmers of strategy for seasoned  players to take advantage of  with two dozen types of stages in  rotation four guys features a lot of  variety to each of its matches  although you'll encounter a race to kick  off each game each one is presented with  different obstacles and  elements that make them feel distinct  from each other the whirligig for  example is one of the longest you'll  encounter  featuring spinning fans and numerous  platforms with rotating hazards  ready to knock you off course by  contrast doordash is a much shorter race  filled with rows of closed doors with a  random few opening once players have  dived through them  the hunt for single routes through each  stage of doors create hilarious  bottlenecks through doorways not  equipped with the stress  sending players tumbling to the floor as  the group attempts to stampede to the  end  it's ridiculous but equally hilarious  every time  other survival based modes are equally  frantic and stressful  rollout for example puts all players on  a string of rotating platforms with  random walls  obstacles and gaps thrown in as they  spin around  moving between each platform while  avoiding the hordes of other players  trying to do the same is as  comical as it is strategic these  survival modes don't require the most  dexterous platforming generally ending  well before things speed up to an  uncomfortable  pace but the way they force all players  to get uncomfortably close to one  another and  so random chaos is extremely effective  if you've ever watched wipeout or  takeshi's castle you'll recognize the  slapstick nature to each stage and  appreciate the care given to balance  elements of luck and skill  with only a handful missing this mark  tail tag gives some players tails and  tasks everyone else with hunting them  down and yanking it off for themselves  it's a fun game of tag on paper but with  each player moving the same speed and  the distance required to latch on and  steal a tail being so small  it quickly becomes a frustrating  runaround it's even worse when used as  the match type for the final stage where  the only player at the end with a tail  wins the whole thing this stage is too  large for the handful of players left in  the game giving an unfair advantage to  the one that happens to start with the  tail  not all of the finales in fall guys  faces these issues however with two  standing out  hexagon is a nerve-wracking platforming  test where the ground disappears beneath  you as you run over it  it's a game where you're rewarded for  staying on each level of platforms for  as long as possible either by making  smart jumps across gaps made by  other players or navigating  intelligently around each level  to stay above the pit of slime below  similarly jump showdown puts all players  on a single platform with two spinning  beams  they move at different speeds forcing  you to continue moving and time your  jumps so that you aren't caught out when  both beams line up  both modes involve a level of skill that  make them more suitable for determining  the overall winner of each game  you'll frequently be thrown into  team-based modes too which can either  delight or dishearten based on who  you're paired up with  a race to get a giant ball through an  obstacle course can become a test of  patience when just one or two teammates  aren't pulling  their weight while rocket league-styled  games of oversized football can feel  extremely one-sided for the same reasons  these aren't faults of the game modes  themselves  most capture the same goofy fun of the  solo ones but when your entire team is  eliminated thanks to just one  or two players it does take some of the  excitement out of immediately jumping  into another game  with its short games and easy to grasp  mechanics four guys is easily the most  approachable and welcoming battle royale  yet  its bursting color and varied game modes  do a good job of reeling you in and  keeping you hooked even if a handful  feel at odds with the easy going nature  of its premise  it can be disheartening to have a game  end prematurely due to uneven team  matches and worse still when you're  forced into one of four guys  unsatisfying finale modes but neither  are enough to derail the fun fall guys  consistently generates  with its chaotic obstacle courses and  ear worm soundtrack ensuring you'll be  coming back  for more  [Music]
 

